---
description: General engineering rules that apply across the codebase
version: 1.7.1
alwaysApply: true
---
- **Be explicit about what has and has not been done**: When responding to user requests, clearly separate completed actions from pending ones. Avoid ambiguity. For each action you describe, state its status explicitly:
  - **Code changes**: Say whether you only edited files (e.g. "Coded but not run") or also executed/verified (e.g. "Coded and ran successfully").
  - **Git**: Say whether changes are committed or not (e.g. "Not yet committed" or "Committed as abc123").
  - **Deployment**: Say whether changes are deployed or not (e.g. "Not deployed; push to main to trigger deploy" or "Deployed via workflow run X").
  Use a brief status section at the end of your response when relevant (e.g. "**Status:** Coded ✓ | Run ✓ | Committed ✗ | Deployed ✗").
- **Always keep software and dependencies up to date (latest-first):** Before adding or bumping a dependency, look up the **latest stable** registry version and try that first. Only step back a major/minor when latest fails peer/engine checks or a documented regression — and note why. Deprecated `npm warn` lines during install are defects: upgrade the parent that pulls the EOL tree (see `.cursor/rules/dependency-freshness.mdc`). When touching package.json, requirements*.txt, or lockfiles, run audits and upgrades; prefer Dependabot/Renovate; merge or fix promptly.

- Keep README and relevant docs in sync with behavior changes.
- Prefer small, focused changes; include or update tests when behavior shifts.
- Write clear commit messages in imperative mood and meaningful PR descriptions.
- Do not commit secrets or credentials; use environment variables and secret storage.
- Add pragmatic logging at boundaries, avoiding sensitive data and excessive noise.
- Gate merges with automated secret scanning and dependency/audit checks; document rotation cadences for any exposed credentials.
